Dawn
[trigger none]
[vignette dungeons/relay_post]

FIXME(mundane): The wind drops in the small hours. By first light the murk has thinned enough to see the road again. Inside the relay post, Devra is at her desk where you left her and Ossal is still in the back room. Neither of them looks up.
# --- COLOR ---
# The brass cylinder on her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth from repeated handling.
# The pot of tea on the brazier is cold, its surface crusted with a thin film of dried leaves.
# The wire receiver on the desk is silent, its indicator light dark, though the cord remains plugged into the wall junction.
# The ledger on the desk contains a fresh entry crossed out with a single line, the ink still damp.
# A dust mask hangs on a hook by the door, its fabric stiff with grit and layered with fine powder.
# The second notebook, Devra's private log, lies open on the desk, its pages filled with marginal annotations in red ink.
# --- end ---
# --- FACTUAL ---
# The wind drops in the small hours. By first light the murk has thinned enough to see the road again. Inside the relay post, Devra is at her desk where you left her and Ossal is still in the back room. Neither of them looks up. The brass cylinder on her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth from repeated handling. The pot of tea on the brazier is cold, its surface crusted with a thin film of dried leaves. The wire receiver on the desk is silent, its indicator light dark, though the cord remains plugged into the wall junction. The ledger on the desk contains a fresh entry crossed out with a single line, the ink still damp. A dust mask hangs on a hook by the door, its fabric stiff with grit and layered with fine powder. The second notebook, Devra's private log, lies open on the desk, its pages filled with marginal annotations in red ink.
# --- end ---
# --- VOICED HPL mundane ---
# The wind dropped in the small hours, as though the very breath of the world had been stilled. By first light, the murk had thinned enough to make out the road again—pale, winding, and ghostly beneath a sky of ashen gray. Inside the relay post, Devra sat at her desk as you had left her, her posture rigid and unchanging, her eyes fixed upon some inward vision. Ossal remained in the back room, motionless, his silence deeper than the quiet around him. The brass cylinder on her desk bore the mark of Kesharat—its surface worn smooth with a thousand touches, polished by fingers that had long since ceased to wonder what they were sealing. The pot of tea on the brazier was cold, its surface encrusted with a thin, brittle film of dried leaves, as if the liquid had been abandoned mid-pour and forgotten for days. The wire receiver lay silent, its indicator light dark, though the cord remained firmly plugged into the wall junction, as though some distant voice had simply ceased to speak. The ledger on the desk bore a fresh entry, neatly inscribed, then crossed out with a single, decisive line—the ink still wet, the stroke too sharp to have been made hours ago. A dust mask hung on a hook by the door, its fabric stiff and brittle with accumulated grit, layered with a fine, pale powder that shimmered faintly in the dim light. And beside it, open upon the desk, lay the second notebook—Devra's private log—its pages filled with frantic marginal annotations in red ink, the script jagged and hurried, as if written in the grip of a terror too deep for words.
# --- end ---
# --- VOICED REH mundane ---
# The wind died at the small hours, leaving the world hushed as if holding its breath. By first light, the murk had thinned enough to make out the road again—washed pale under a sky the color of old iron. Inside the relay post, Devra sat at her desk just as you'd left her, her fingers still poised over the ledger, her face a mask of weary focus. Ossal remained in the back room, his presence a quiet weight in the stillness. The brass cylinder on her desk bore the mark of Kesharat—its surface worn smooth from a thousand anxious hands, a seal that had seen too many secrets pass through. The tea on the brazier was cold, its surface crusted with a brittle film of dried leaves, long since gone to dust. The wire receiver sat silent, its indicator dark, though the cord still bit into the wall junction like a vulture's beak. The ledger held a fresh entry, crossed out with a single, sharp line—ink still wet, as if the hand that wrote it had not yet cooled. A dust mask hung by the door, its fabric stiff with grit, layered with fine powder that clung like ash from a dead fire. And beside it, Devra's private log lay open, its pages thick with red-ink annotations—sharp, urgent, like wounds in the paper.
# --- end ---
# --- CRITIC factual ---
# (no findings)
# --- end ---
# --- CRITIC voiced HPL mundane ---
# [CRIT] "her posture rigid and unchanging, her eyes fixed upon some inward vision" — PC INTERIORITY and INVENTION. The RECORD states Devra "is at her desk" with no detail of posture or gaze direction.
# [CRIT] "motionless, his silence deeper than the quiet around him" — INVENTION. The RECORD only states Ossal "is still in the back room"; no physical description of motionlessness or comparative silence quality is given.
# [CRIT] "polished by fingers that had long since ceased to wonder what they were sealing" — INVENTION. Introduces a speculative history and emotional state ungrounded in the RECORD.
# [CRIT] "as if the liquid had been abandoned mid-pour and forgotten for days" — INVENTION and LOADED EMBELLISHMENT. The RECORD states the pot is cold with a crusted surface; the draft adds duration ("days") and narrative framing ("abandoned mid-pour") not in the source.
# [CRIT] "as though some distant voice had simply ceased to speak" — INVENTION. The RECORD notes the receiver is silent with dark indicator light; this adds a metaphorical claim about a voice ceasing that is not grounded in fact.
# [CRIT] "the ink still wet, the stroke too sharp to have been made hours ago" — CONTRADICTION and LOADED EMBELLISHMENT. The RECORD says "ink still damp"; "wet" and "the stroke too sharp" assert a more recent timing than the RECORD permits.
# [CRIT] "layered with a fine, pale powder that shimmered faintly in the dim light" — INVENTION. The RECORD describes "fine powder" but adds color ("pale") and optical property ("shimmered faintly").
# [CRIT] "frantic marginal annotations" and "the script jagged and hurried, as if written in the grip of a terror too deep for words" — INVENTION and PC INTERIORITY. The RECORD states "marginal annotations in red ink" only; the draft adds emotional urgency and psychological state.
# --- end ---
# --- CRITIC voiced REH mundane ---
# [CRIT] "her fingers still poised over the ledger, her face a mask of weary focus" — PC INTERIORITY and INVENTION. The RECORD states Devra "is at her desk" but does not specify hand position or facial expression.
# [CRIT] "his presence a quiet weight in the stillness" — PC INTERIORITY. This narrates the player character's perception/interpretation of Ossal's presence, not a fact about Ossal.
# [LOW] "from a thousand anxious hands" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT. The RECORD says "repeated handling"; "thousand anxious hands" introduces both quantity and emotional state not in the source.
# [CRIT] "long since gone to dust" — CONTRADICTION. The RECORD describes the tea surface as "crusted with a thin film of dried leaves," not dust.
# [LOW] "like a vulture's beak" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT. The RECORD states the cord "remains plugged into the wall junction"; the simile introduces a predatory, sinister quality not present in the source.
# [LOW] "ink still wet, as if the hand that wrote it had not yet cooled" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT. The RECORD says "ink still damp"; "wet" and the speculative clause about the hand's temperature add interpretive weight beyond the fact.
# [LOW] "like wounds in the paper" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT. The RECORD states the pages are "filled with marginal annotations in red ink"; the wound metaphor introduces violence/injury implication not in the source.
# --- end ---

choices:

* Step back onto the road
    FIXME(mundane): You take your pack down from the peg, set the door on its latch so it does not slam, and step out into the cleared morning. Whatever happens between the two of them in this small stone building, it happens without your part in it.
    # --- COLOR ---
    # The brass cylinder on her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn from repeated use.
    # The pot of tea on the brazier is cold, its surface film cracked like dried mud.
    # The wire receiver on the desk is silent, its brass casing scuffed from repeated handling.
    # The stack of filed transcriptions on the desk includes a folder labeled "Resonance Platform - Mesa Sector 7."
    # The pencil-stub on the desk is worn to a nub, its tip split from repeated sharpening.
    # The back room door is slightly ajar, revealing a narrow cot and a stack of unopened census forms.
    # --- end ---
    # --- FACTUAL ---
    # You take your pack down from the peg, set the door on its latch so it does not slam, and step out into the cleared morning. The brass cylinder on her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn from repeated use. The pot of tea on the brazier is cold, its surface film cracked like dried mud. The wire receiver on the desk is silent, its brass casing scuffed from repeated handling. The stack of filed transcriptions on the desk includes a folder labeled "Resonance Platform - Mesa Sector 7." The pencil-stub on the desk is worn to a nub, its tip split from repeated sharpening. The back room door is slightly ajar, revealing a narrow cot and a stack of unopened census forms. Whatever happens between the two of them in this small stone building, it happens without your part in it.
    # --- end ---
    # --- VOICED HPL mundane ---
    # You take your pack down from the peg, set the door on its latch with a careful click so it does not slam, and step out into the cleared morning—thin, pale, and still with the hush of something just waking. The brass cylinder upon her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth and pitted with the scars of countless rotations, as if the very metal had absorbed the weight of secret words. The pot of tea on the brazier lies cold, its surface cracked into a network of fine fissures like dried mud on a sun-baked plain, and the faintest ghost of steam still curls from its rim, though it has long since ceased to breathe. The wire receiver on the desk is silent, its brass casing scuffed and dented from repeated handling—each scratch a record of a voice that once spoke through it, now gone. The stack of filed transcriptions upon the desk bears the label "Resonance Platform - Mesa Sector 7," the ink faded and the edges frayed from frequent retrieval. The pencil-stub lies half-buried in the dust, worn to a nub, its tip split and splintered from the endless, anxious act of sharpening. The back room door is slightly ajar, revealing only a narrow cot with a blanket folded with military precision and a small, untouched stack of census forms—each sheet blank, each name yet unclaimed. Whatever passes between the two of them in this small stone building, whatever quiet exchange of glances, of half-uttered truths, of glimmering understandings, it happens without your part in it. You are only the listener, the watcher, the one left standing in the thin light, hearing the silence as a voice.
    # --- end ---
    # --- VOICED REH mundane ---
    # You took your pack from the peg, the leather worn smooth from years of travel, and set the door on its latch with a quiet click so it would not slam. The morning air outside was clear and sharp, the kind that bites at the lungs and stings the eyes. On the desk, the brass cylinder of the Kesharat cipher seal lay dull and scarred, its surface worn thin by the grip of countless hands. The tea in the brazier was cold, its surface cracked like sun-baked earth, a dried crust of neglect. The wire receiver sat silent, its brass casing scraped raw from constant use, the contacts fouled with dust and time. The stack of transcriptions bore the label—"Resonance Platform - Mesa Sector 7"—in cramped, hurried script. A pencil-stub, split at the tip and worn to a nub, lay half-buried in the inkwell. The door to the back room stood ajar, revealing a narrow cot, a blanket crumpled like a dead thing, and a pile of census forms untouched, their edges crisp and unbroken. Whatever passed between them in this stone-walled room, whatever secrets were spoken or left unspoken, you were not part of it. You stood outside the circle, a shadow in the morning light, and the silence held your name like a weight.
    # --- end ---
    # --- CRITIC factual ---
    # (no findings)
    # --- end ---
    # --- CRITIC voiced HPL mundane ---
