    @if tag relay_post.predicted_arrival {
        FIXME(dread): What you carry from the night is one thing. A maintenance notice on the wire, timestamped before you decided to shelter at the relay post, naming the relay's coordinates and predicting the arrival of transient personnel. No origin header. The message did not come from the capital. The system knew you were coming. The system does not know anything. Both of these are true.
        # --- COLOR ---
        # The brass cylinder on her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth from repeated use.
        # The wire receiver on the desk is silent but still warm to the touch, as if recently active.
        # The pot of tea on the brazier is cold, its surface crusted with a thin film of dried leaves.
        # A set of Kesharat-issued census forms lies open on the desk, one corner folded back with a pencil mark.
        # The ledger on the desk contains a fresh entry crossed out in red ink, the original text illegible.
        # The wind outside has lessened, but the dust still clings to the stone walls in fine, rust-colored layers.
        # --- end ---
        # --- FACTUAL ---
        # The brass cylinder on her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth from repeated use. The wire receiver on the desk is silent but still warm to the touch, as if recently active. The pot of tea on the brazier is cold, its surface crusted with a thin film of dried leaves. A set of Kesharat-issued census forms lies open on the desk, one corner folded back with a pencil mark. The ledger on the desk contains a fresh entry crossed out in red ink, the original text illegible. The wind outside has lessened, but the dust still clings to the stone walls in fine, rust-colored layers. The maintenance notice on the wire, timestamped before you decided to shelter at the relay post, names the relay's coordinates and predicts the arrival of transient personnel. No origin header. The message did not come from the capital. The system knew you were coming. The system does not know anything. Both of these are true.
        # --- end ---
        # --- VOICED HPL dread ---
        # The brass cylinder on her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth by a thousand anxious hands, its engravings now so blurred they seem to writhe when stared at too long. The wire receiver lies silent, yet warm—warm as though a living thing had just withdrawn from it, leaving behind the faint, metallic scent of scorched copper. The pot of tea on the brazier is cold, its surface cracked with a thin, brittle film of dried leaves, as if the liquid had been left to steep beyond time, beyond reason. A set of Kesharat-issued census forms lies open upon the desk, one corner folded back with a pencil mark—yet no name is written beside it, and the columns of data end abruptly, as if the scribe had been interrupted mid-sentence by something that would not be named. The ledger beside it bears a fresh entry, hastily scrawled in ink that runs like blood, then violently crossed out in red—so violently that the paper is torn through, and beneath the smears, the original text is not merely illegible, but *wrong*, as though written in a language that should not exist, or in a hand that was not human.
        #
        # Outside, the wind has lessened, but the dust lingers—fine, rust-colored dust that clings to the stone walls like the skin of a dead thing, and that glimmers faintly in the dim light, as if stirred by some unseen breath. The maintenance notice on the wire, timestamped before you decided to shelter at the relay post, lists the relay's coordinates and predicts the arrival of transient personnel—yet there is no origin header, no identifying signal, no trace of source. The message did not come from the capital. And yet the system knew you were coming. The system does not know anything. Both truths are true. And now, in the silence between heartbeats, you hear it: a soft, rhythmic ticking from within the cylinder, as if something inside had begun to turn, though no key has been turned, and no mechanism has been wound.
        # --- end ---
        # --- VOICED REH dread ---
        # The brass cylinder on her desk was a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth by the touch of a thousand hands, each one seeking secrets they were not meant to know. The wire receiver sat cold, yet still warm to the touch—as if the voice that had spoken through it had only just fallen silent. The pot of tea upon the brazier was stone-cold, its surface crusted with a film of dried leaves, like the skin of some dead thing. Open upon the desk lay a set of Kesharat-issued census forms, one corner folded back with a pencil mark, as though someone had paused mid-entry, then vanished. The ledger bore a fresh entry, crossed out in red ink—so freshly crossed that the blood-red line still glistened, like a wound. The original words were gone, but not erased. They *fought* to remain. Outside, the wind had stilled, but the dust clung to the stone walls in fine, rust-colored layers, as if the very air had turned to ash. The maintenance notice on the wire—timestamped before you ever turned toward this relay post—named the relay's coordinates and foretold the arrival of transient personnel. No origin header. The message did not come from the capital. And yet, the system knew you were coming. The system does not know anything. Both truths stood, unblinking, in the hollow silence. One could not be true without the other. And that was the dread.
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC factual ---
        # (no findings)
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC voiced HPL dread ---
        # [CRIT] "worn smooth by a thousand anxious hands" — INVENTION: the thousand hands are not in the RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "its engravings now so blurred they seem to writhe when stared at too long" — INVENTION: no engravings mentioned in RECORD; the writhing effect is added sensory invention.
        # [CRIT] "warm as though a living thing had just withdrawn from it, leaving behind the faint, metallic scent of scorched copper" — INVENTION: the simile and scent are added; RECORD says only "warm to the touch, as if recently active."
        # [CRIT] "its surface cracked with a thin, brittle film" — CONTRADICTION: RECORD says "thin film" but not "cracked"; this adds a structural detail not present.
        # [CRIT] "as if the liquid had been left to steep beyond time, beyond reason" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: adds metaphysical dread claim not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "yet no name is written beside it, and the columns of data end abruptly, as if the scribe had been interrupted mid-sentence by something that would not be named" — INVENTION: RECORD does not mention names, columns ending abruptly, or interruption.
        # [CRIT] "hastily scrawled in ink that runs like blood" — INVENTION: "hastily scrawled" and the simile are not in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "so violently that the paper is torn through" — INVENTION: RECORD says only "crossed out in red ink"; no tearing mentioned.
        # [CRIT] "the original text is not merely illegible, but *wrong*, as though written in a language that should not exist, or in a hand that was not human" — INVENTION: RECORD says only "illegible"; the "wrong language" and inhuman hand are inventions.
        # [CRIT] "like the skin of a dead thing, and that glimmers faintly in the dim light, as if stirred by some unseen breath" — INVENTION: simile and shimmer/breath effects added; RECORD describes dust only as "fine, rust-colored layers."
        # [CRIT] "yet there is no origin header, no identifying signal, no trace of source" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: RECORD says "No origin header" once; repetition with "no identifying signal, no trace of source" adds interpretive intensity as fact.
        # [CRIT] "And now, in the silence between heartbeats, you hear it: a soft, rhythmic ticking from within the cylinder, as if something inside had begun to turn, though no key has been turned, and no mechanism has been wound" — PC INTERIORITY + INVENTION: "you hear it" places the PC in a perceptual/temporal state ("in the silence between heartbeats") not in RECORD; the ticking from inside the cylinder is entirely invented.
        # --- end ---
        # --- CRITIC voiced REH dread ---
        # [CRIT] "worn smooth by the touch of a thousand hands, each one seeking secrets they were not meant to know" — INVENTION: adds narrative about prior users and their intentions beyond "repeated use."
        # [CRIT] "The wire receiver sat cold, yet still warm to the touch—as if the voice that had spoken through it had only just fallen silent." — CONTRADICTION: RECORD states receiver is "silent but still warm"; VOICED adds it is "cold" which directly contradicts "warm."
        # [LOW] "like the skin of some dead thing" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: adds a specific simile introducing a death metaphor not in RECORD.
        # [LOW] "as though someone had paused mid-entry, then vanished" — INVENTION: speculates about human action (pausing, vanishing) not stated in RECORD.
        # [CRIT] "so freshly crossed that the blood-red line still glistened, like a wound" — INVENTION: adds sensory claim (glistening) and wound metaphor not in RECORD, which only says "fresh entry crossed out."
        # [LOW] "The original words were gone, but not erased. They *fought* to remain." — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: personification ("fought") adds agency/emotion to the text beyond RECORD's "illegible."
        # [LOW] "as if the very air had turned to ash" — LOADED EMBELLISHMENT: adds metaphorical transformation not present in RECORD description of dust.
